  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The design and implementation of policies to tackle informality and protect workers is improved</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">According to ILO data, in 2022, 37.1% of Georgia&apos;s employment was informal, largely driven by informal work in agriculture. The informal economy in Georgia has declined due to comprehensive reforms implemented over the past decades.
ILO intervention aims to enhance Georgia&apos;s capacity to foster the transition to formality through the design and implementation of a National Integrated and Gender-responsive Formalization Strategy (NFS). The strategy first focuses on creating evidence for policy makers. Given the lack of a comprehensive analysis of the prevalence and drivers of informality in the country, the ILO supports the development of a diagnosis using updated statistical standards on the informal economy. This is followed by an in-depth analysis of key bottlenecks encountered, such as productivity, taxation and social protection, among others. The NFS will be drafted based on these findings. This strategy incorporates inputs from the relevant stakeholders, including the policy positions of social partners, and will be the basis for a National Formalization Programme (NFP). Moreover, it is crucial to enhance the capacity of relevant institutions and social partners to actively support the implementation of the NFP. Here, the role of the Labor Inspectorate, reinstated in 2015, is paramount. Presently, labour inspection overlooks informal work during company visits, highlighting the need to develop a comprehensive training and support programme. This initiative will support labour inspectors to effectively monitor informal employment, thereby bolstering a pivotal aspect of the NFP.</narrative>
  </description>
